WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:03.930
Identity design should be part of MCP planning from the start.

00:00:03.930 --> 00:00:07.411
An integration may act as the user, as the application,

00:00:07.411 --> 00:00:09.152
or as a service account.

00:00:09.152 --> 00:00:12.285
Those choices affect auditability, least privilege, and user expectations.

00:00:12.285 --> 00:00:14.722
User identity answers who requested the work.

00:00:14.722 --> 00:00:18.551
Application identity answers which host or product is making the call.

00:00:18.551 --> 00:00:22.033
Service identity answers what backend component is performing the integration.

00:00:22.033 --> 00:00:25.862
Good design keeps these identities distinct enough to reason about authorization.

00:00:25.862 --> 00:00:30.040
Delegated access is a common pattern where an application acts on behalf

00:00:30.040 --> 00:00:32.129
of a user within defined limits.

00:00:32.129 --> 00:00:34.914
Service accounts may be appropriate for system-level workflows,

00:00:34.914 --> 00:00:37.699
but they should be narrowly scoped and monitored.

00:00:37.699 --> 00:00:42.225
Secrets and tokens should be protected from prompts, logs, and unnecessary tool exposure.

00:00:42.225 --> 00:00:44.314
Least privilege means each server, tool,

00:00:44.314 --> 00:00:48.143
or credential receives only the permissions needed for its intended use,

00:00:48.143 --> 00:00:50.928
in the intended context, for the intended users.

00:00:50.928 --> 00:00:54.061
Consent and user approval are different from model preference.

00:00:54.061 --> 00:00:56.150
A model may propose an action,

00:00:56.150 --> 00:01:01.720
but the application should verify whether the action is allowed and whether the user intended it.

00:01:01.720 --> 00:01:05.202
For low-risk read-only requests, policy may allow execution without interruption.

00:01:05.202 --> 00:01:06.246
For higher-impact actions,

00:01:06.246 --> 00:01:10.424
the user may need a clear approval prompt showing what will happen.

00:01:10.424 --> 00:01:12.861
Approval should be specific enough to matter,

00:01:12.861 --> 00:01:15.646
not a vague blanket acceptance of future activity.

00:01:15.646 --> 00:01:17.735
Tenant and workspace boundaries also matter.

00:01:17.735 --> 00:01:21.912
A user in one workspace should not gain access to another workspace

00:01:21.912 --> 00:01:24.698
because a tool or resource is broadly connected.

00:01:24.698 --> 00:01:27.831
A model-generated action request should not bypass application authorization.

00:01:27.831 --> 00:01:32.357
MCP integrations should not rely on the model as the final permission system.

00:01:32.357 --> 00:01:37.230
The stable rule is simple: the model can help decide what might be useful,

00:01:37.230 --> 00:01:41.060
but identity-aware application logic and downstream policy decide what is allowed.
